/-! # The quasi-compact open locus of closed embeddings from a finite locally free source

Source: Conrad, Reductive Group Schemes, Lemma 2.3.4, proof, printed p.70 (open monicity
input); the arbitrary-base finite-coordinate argument recorded in the DAG uses Stacks
Tags 02KB, 00DX, 00DF, 0C3F, 07Z8, 07ZA, 00E4, 00E8 and 01QO.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-locally-free-closed-embedding-locus)
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- For a finite locally free source and an affine finitely presented target over `P`,
closed immersion after base change is represented by a quasi-compact open subscheme.
Its points are exactly the closed-immersion fibres, and its inverse image along every
base change represents the same condition for the base-changed morphism. Quasi-compactness
is a property of the inclusion, without assuming that the base itself is quasi-compact.

Source: Conrad, Reductive Group Schemes, Lemma 2.3.4, proof, printed p.70, open monicity
input, generalized by the finite-coordinate proof in the DAG (Stacks Tags 02KB, 00DX,
00DF, 0C3F, 05P8, 07Z8, 07ZA, 00E4, 00E8, 01I4 and 01QO).
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-locally-free-closed-embedding-locus)

Proof:
1. By lem-sc-finite-pushforward-locally-free, trivialize the structure-algebra
   pushforward on affine charts U = Spec A. Finite and affine morphisms give
   D_U = Spec B and X_U = Spec C; B is free of rank n. By
   lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-opens-finite-presentation, C has finitely many algebra
   generators x_i and relations. Write b_i for their images in B. For n = 0, B = 0
   and all scalar extensions of C → B are surjective, so the local locus is U.
2. For n > 0, lem-weil-cayley-hamilton-free-module applied to multiplication by b_i
   gives a monic relation of degree n. Induction on total degree reduces every monomial
   to the finite list w_e = product b_i^(e_i), with every e_i < n. These monomials
   span precisely the image of C → B as an A-module; if there are no generators the
   list consists of the empty product 1. No submodule finiteness assertion is needed.
3. Map the free module on that finite list to B and call its cokernel Q. By
   lem-weil-pbc-tensor-right-exact, Q tensor R is the cokernel of the same matrix over
   every A-algebra R. The image of C tensor R → B tensor R is exactly the R-span of
   w_e tensor 1, so surjectivity is equivalent to Q tensor R = 0, without flatness.
4. The quotient Q is finite type. By lem-weil-pbc-spec-module-tilde-sections and
   lem-trf-fitting-ideal-generate-locally at r = 0, the complement W_U of the support
   of Fit_0(tilde Q) represents vanishing of its pullback. The tensor identification
   of lem-trf-fitting-sheaf-arbitrary-pullback and sections on D(1) identify this on
   affine tests with Q tensor R = 0.
5. By lem-trf-fitting-ideal-sheaf-affine-sections, Fit_0 is generated by determinants
   of n kernel vectors of A^n → Q. Each is a linear combination of the columns of
   the finite monomial matrix. Expand the Leibniz formula from
   def-weil-pbc-matrix-determinant in all columns: these determinants are generated
   by the finite list of n-column minors. Conversely every such column is in the
   kernel. Thus W_U is the finite union of the principal opens of these minors.
6. Each principal open is a localization spectrum by
   lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open and is quasi-compact by
   lem-weil-pbc-spec-basic-opens-unit-ideal-quasi-compact. Finite unions are
   quasi-compact, including the empty union. The rank-zero locus is all of U.
7. By lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra, f_R is Spec of the tensor map.
   The affine criterion in def-weil-closed-immersion identifies its being a closed
   immersion with surjectivity. Residue-field tests make the W_U agree on overlaps;
   their union V is an open subscheme by def-weil-open-immersion.
8. On any affine open of P, refine the trivializing cover by principal opens and
   choose finitely many using spectrum quasi-compactness. By
   lem-pqf-finite-affine-cover-qc-open take finite affine refinements. Step 6 makes
   each intersection with V quasi-compact; their finite union is quasi-compact.
   This is QuasiCompact V.ι, as in def-weil-finite-type-morphism.
9. For any T → P, cover T by affines mapping into these charts. Factoring through
   V is local, and closed immersion is local on the target: closedness of the image,
   the embedding property and surjectivity of the structure-sheaf map all glue on
   this cover. The affine equivalences give the universal factorization criterion
   and the residue-field description.
10. For any P' → P, the inverse-image open represents factorization of a composite
    through V. Associativity of fibre products, def-pc-fibre-product, identifies
    the resulting map with base change of f_P'. Hence this inverse-image open is
    the new locus; the finite-chart argument also gives its quasi-compact inclusion.
    Empty schemes, rank-zero charts and nonreduced rings cause no exception. -/
theorem exists_finiteLocallyFree_closedEmbeddingLocus {P D X : Scheme.{u}}
    (d : D ⟶ P) (x : X ⟶ P) (f : D ⟶ X) (hf : f ≫ x = d)
    [d.IsFiniteLocallyFree] [IsAffineHom x] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation x]
    [QuasiCompact x] [QuasiSeparated x] :
    ∃ V : P.Opens, QuasiCompact V.ι ∧
      (∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (t : T ⟶ P),
        (∃ l : T ⟶ V.toScheme, l ≫ V.ι = t) ↔
          IsClosedImmersion (pullback.map d t x t f (𝟙 T) (𝟙 P)
            (by simpa only [Category.comp_id] using hf.symm) (by simp))) ∧
      (∀ p : P, p ∈ V ↔
        IsClosedImmersion
          (pullback.map d (P.fromSpecResidueField p) x (P.fromSpecResidueField p)
            f (𝟙 (Spec (P.residueField p))) (𝟙 P)
            (by simpa only [Category.comp_id] using hf.symm) (by simp))) ∧
      (∀ (P' : Scheme.{u}) (b : P' ⟶ P),
        QuasiCompact (b ⁻¹ᵁ V).ι ∧
          ∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (t : T ⟶ P'),
            (∃ l : T ⟶ (b ⁻¹ᵁ V).toScheme, l ≫ (b ⁻¹ᵁ V).ι = t) ↔
              IsClosedImmersion
                (pullback.map (pullback.snd d b) t (pullback.snd x b) t
                  (pullback.map d b x b f (𝟙 P') (𝟙 P)
                    (by simpa only [Category.comp_id] using hf.symm) (by simp))
                  (𝟙 T) (𝟙 P') (by simp [pullback.map]) (by simp))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
